# android.hardware.memtrack@1.0-service with memtrack.mt6797.so.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY LEFT DENIED. This file intentionally contains no allow rule.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# FACT (live device, 2026-08-10), fires on every stats poll:
#   avc: denied { read open getattr } for
#        path="/sys/kernel/debug/mali0/gpu_memory"
#        scontext=u:r:hal_memtrack_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:debugfs:s0
#        tclass=file  permissive=1                                     (x52)
#   avc: denied { read open } for path="/sys/kernel/debug/ion/clients"
#        scontext=u:r:hal_memtrack_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:debugfs:s0
#        tclass=dir  permissive=1
#
# FACT: debugfs IS mounted on this build. /proc/mounts carries
#   debugfs /sys/kernel/debug debugfs rw,seclabel,relatime 0 0
# put there on purpose by rootdir/init.mt6797.rc:38 (`mount debugfs debugfs
# /sys/kernel/debug` at early-init), whose own comment names this HAL as one of
# the two reasons it was restored. Both nodes exist and are u:object_r:debugfs:s0
# (verified with ls -ldZ).
#
# FACT: the blob really does want them. `strings /vendor/lib64/hw/memtrack.mt6797.so`
# contains "/d/ion/clients/", "/d/ion/clients/%s", "/sys/kernel/debug/mali0/gpu_memory",
# "ion_mm_heap", "ion_mm_heap_for_camera", and the error string
# "%s error to open /sys/kernel/debug/ion/clients/%d: %s" — i.e. the failure is
# a logged branch in the blob, not a crash.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY BREAKS UNDER ENFORCING: a number in `dumpsys meminfo`, and
# nothing else. The whole chain swallows the error by design:
#   - hardware/interfaces/memtrack/1.0/default/Memtrack.cpp:41-72 —
#     getMemory() calls _hidl_cb(MemtrackStatus::SUCCESS, records) on EVERY
#     path. When mModule->getMemory returns non-zero it simply returns an empty
#     records vector, still tagged SUCCESS.
#   - the only consumer is frameworks/base/core/jni/android_os_Debug.cpp:200
#     read_memtrack_memory(), which on error does ALOGW("failed to get memory
#     consumption info: %d") and returns. Nothing retries, nothing fails.
# So the observable consequence is that the Graphics/GL lines of `dumpsys
# meminfo` and procstats read 0. No allocation fails, no process is affected,
# no frame is dropped.
#
# WHY NO RULE: debugfs is a whole-kernel debug surface under one label —
# wakeup_sources, tracing, the ION client dump, the Mali allocator dump, every
# driver's private files. There is no genfscon that can carve
# /sys/kernel/debug/mali0/gpu_memory out of it, because debugfs is labelled by
# its mount and genfscon on debugfs would re-type whole subtrees, so the
# narrowest workable rule here is still `allow hal_memtrack_default debugfs:file
# r_file_perms` — a HAL gets read of the entire kernel debug filesystem. AOSP's
# own position is the same: private/domain.te dontaudits debugfs access for
# coredomain precisely because it is expected to be absent, and public/
# hal_memtrack.te grants nothing beyond binder.
#
# Opening debugfs to a HAL to repair a dumpsys statistic is a bad trade. Left
# denied. Under enforcing the reads fail, the blob takes its logged error
# branch, memtrack reports empty, and that is the correct outcome.
#
# Separately worth knowing (not a sepolicy issue): init.mt6797.rc:38 mounts
# debugfs at early-init on a user-facing build largely for this HAL and for
# KernelWakelockReader. If the wakelock reader turns out not to need it either,
# the mount itself is the thing to remove, not the label.
#
# REVISIT IF: someone shows a real regression that is not a statistic — e.g.
# lowmemorykiller or the framework's memory-pressure logic consuming
# memtrack_proc_graphics_pss() and changing behaviour when it reads 0. On this
# tree it does not: android_os_Debug.cpp is the only caller.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADDENDUM (core-domain sweep, same day): the decision above is not merely the
# better trade — the rule it declines to write is NOT AVAILABLE. It would fail
# the build.
#
# FACT, system/sepolicy/public/domain.te:1310-1316, verbatim:
#   # Do not allow access to the generic debugfs label. This is too broad.
#   # Instead, if access to part of debugfs is desired, it should have a
#   # more specific label.
#   # TODO: fix system_server and dumpstate
#   neverallow { domain -init -vendor_init -system_server -dumpstate }
#       debugfs:file no_rw_file_perms;
#
# hal_memtrack_default is not on that exemption list, and unlike the
# coredomain debugfs neverallow in private/domain.te:57-61 this one sits in
# public/ outside any full_treble_only()/not_full_treble() guard, so it applies
# to this build regardless of ro.treble.enabled (which is false here). Anyone
# who later "just adds the allow rule" gets a neverallow failure out of
# checkpolicy, not a working gpu_memory read.
#
# That also settles the shape of any future fix: it must be a specific type via
# genfscon, exactly as the AOSP comment says, or nothing. Since genfscon on
# debugfs would re-type whole subtrees (see WHY NO RULE above), nothing is the
# answer this device should keep.
